  "brollRead": [
    {
      "rec": {
        "stored": 0
      },
      "id": "bs_ld_01",
      "name": "fingertip pushing on the sticky old foam",
      "desc": "",
      "rowId": "r4",
      "rowIndex": 3,
      "ordinal": 0,
      "markerless": false,
      "orphaned": false,
      "status": "shot",
      "reshoot": false,
      "notes": "",
      "owner": "",
      "audioRef": "First problem. These little foam strips keep light from leaking in round the back door. After forty years they turn into black goo. If you shoot with them like this, every frame gets a red stripe down one side. And you won't find out until the film comes back from the lab, weeks later, which is a very specific kind of heartbreak. There's a second strip up by the hinge, and a thin one under the mirror that stops it slapping the top of the chamber. Those three are the whole job.",
      "location": "",
      "props": [],
      "origin": "script"
    },
    {
      "rec": {
        "stored": 1
      },
      "id": "bs_ld_02",
      "name": "the toothpick lifting a long strand of goo",
      "desc": "",
      "rowId": "r6",
      "rowIndex": 5,
      "ordinal": 0,
      "markerless": false,
      "orphaned": false,
      "status": "shot",
      "reshoot": true,
      "notes": "Soft focus — grab it again on the macro day.",
      "owner": "",
      "audioRef": "This part takes about forty minutes and it is genuinely the worst forty minutes in camera repair. Go slowly. If you scratch the channel, the new seal won't sit flat. Soak a cotton bud, let it sit for a minute, and the old foam lifts out in long sticky strings. Keep a paper towel under the camera, because it drips, and keep the naphtha well away from the lens and the mirror. When the channel is clean, it should be bare shiny metal from end to end.",
      "location": "",
      "props": [],
      "origin": "script"
    },
    {
      "rec": {
        "stored": 2
      },
      "id": "bs_ld_03",
      "name": "slow-motion shutter firing at one second",
      "desc": "",
      "rowId": "r10",
      "rowIndex": 9,
      "ordinal": 0,
      "markerless": false,
      "orphaned": false,
      "status": "unshot",
      "reshoot": false,
      "notes": "",
      "owner": "",
      "audioRef": "The fix everyone suggests is to exercise it. Fire it at every speed, fifty times each, and a lot of the time the old oil loosens up on its own. It's boring, but it's free, and it doesn't involve opening anything. Start at the fastest speed, a thousandth of a second, and work your way down. The fast speeds barely touch the mechanism that's sticking, but they get everything moving. By the time you reach the slow speeds, where the gears actually do the timing, the old oil has warmed up and started to spread.",
      "location": "",
      "props": [],
      "origin": "script"
    },
    {
      "rec": {
        "stored": 3
      },
      "id": "bs_ld_04",
      "name": "battery dropping into the base plate",
