      "audioRef": "Loading a manual camera for the first time is the moment everyone gets nervous. Pull the leader across, hook it into the slot, and wind on until both sets of sprocket holes are caught. Then, and this is the important bit, watch the rewind knob turn as you advance. If it doesn't turn, the film isn't moving. You'll shoot thirty-six frames onto the same patch of film, or onto nothing at all, and you'll only find out when you try to rewind and there's no tension. Ask me how I know. Close the back, fire two blank frames to get past the part you fogged while loading, and the counter should sit on one.",
      "location": "workbench",
      "props": [],
      "origin": "script"
    },
    {
      "rec": {
        "stored": 6
      },
      "id": "bs_ld_07",
      "name": "feet on the gravel path, low angle",
      "desc": "",
      "rowId": "r22",
      "rowIndex": 21,
      "ordinal": 0,
      "markerless": false,
      "orphaned": false,
      "status": "unshot",
      "reshoot": false,
      "notes": "",
      "owner": "",
      "audioRef": "Then I took it for a walk. The whole point of a camera like this is that it slows you down. There's no screen to check. You look at the needle, you turn the rings, and you commit. I set the camera for the light at the start of the walk, and then only changed it when the needle told me to. That meant I spent most of the hour just looking at things, which, honestly, I don't do enough of with a phone in my pocket. By the time I reached the river, I'd only taken four pictures, and I was fine with that.",
      "location": "Riverside park",
      "props": [],
      "origin": "script"
    },
    {
      "rec": {
        "stored": 7
      },
      "id": "bs_ld_08",
      "name": "the heron taking off",
      "desc": "",
      "rowId": "r23",
      "rowIndex": 22,
      "ordinal": 0,
      "markerless": false,
      "orphaned": false,
      "status": "unshot",
      "reshoot": false,
      "notes": "",
      "owner": "",
      "audioRef": "I shot all thirty-six frames in about an hour. Some of them I thought about for a long time. Some of them I just took, because the light was doing something and I didn't want to miss it. The heron stood in the shallows for nearly ten minutes while I crept closer, and then took off the moment I lifted the camera, which I'm told is what herons are for. The bike on the rail was the last frame. I stood there a long time deciding whether it was worth it, and then took it anyway, because it was frame thirty-six.",
      "location": "Riverside park",
      "props": [],
      "origin": "script"
    },
    {
      "rec": {
        "stored": 8
      },
      "id": "bs_ld_09",
      "name": "prints laid out on the table, overhead",
      "desc": "",
      "rowId": "r27",
      "rowIndex": 26,
      "ordinal": 0,
      "markerless": false,
      "orphaned": false,
      "status": "unshot",
      "reshoot": false,
      "notes": "",
      "owner": "",
      "audioRef": "And I'm really happy with them. The meter held up. There's no light leak anywhere, not even on the first frame. Two frames are soft because I missed focus, and that's entirely on me. The heron is out of focus too. The heron was always going to be out of focus. The colours are what got me. Portra does this warm, soft thing with late light that I've spent years trying to get out of a phone with filters, and here it is, straight out of the scanner, no editing at all. Rita was right. It forgave me for everything.",
      "location": "",
      "props": [],
      "origin": "script"
    }
  ],
